(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-musculoskeletal-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Musculoskeletal Physical Examination — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","questions":[{"q":"Normal hip flexion, tested supine by bringing the knee to the chest, is approximately what range?","choices":["About 30 degrees","About 45 to 50 degrees","About 180 degrees","About 110 to 130 degrees"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal hip flexion, tested supine by bringing the knee toward the chest, is approximately 110 to 130 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"Normal hip extension, lifting the leg behind the body, is approximately how many degrees?","choices":["About 90 degrees","About 130 degrees","About 30 degrees","About 5 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal hip extension, lifting the leg behind the body, is approximately 30 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"When testing hip adduction and abduction, what should the examiner do to isolate the movement?","choices":["Stabilize the opposite hip","Have the patient stand on one leg","Fully flex both knees to the chest","Apply the reflex hammer to the tendon"],"answer":0,"exp":"During hip adduction and abduction, the examiner stabilizes the opposite hip to isolate the movement and prevent pelvic tilting. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which structures does the examiner palpate on the posterior hip?","choices":["The patella, tibial tuberosity, and knee joint line","The medial malleolus and lateral malleolus","The greater tubercle and the bicipital groove","The posterior superior iliac spine, sacroiliac joint, and ischial tuberosity"],"answer":3,"exp":"On the posterior hip the examiner palpates the posterior superior iliac spine, the sacroiliac joint, and the ischial tuberosity. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 53"},{"q":"Normal knee flexion is approximately how many degrees?","choices":["About 45 degrees","About 75 degrees","About 130 degrees","About 180 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal knee flexion is approximately 130 degrees, with extension from about 0 to 15 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which of the following is a landmark palpated at the knee joint?","choices":["The greater trochanter","The acromion process","The tibial tuberosity","The medial malleolus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Knee palpation includes the patella and patellar tendon, the medial and lateral femoral condyles, the tibial tuberosity, the tibial plateaus, and the joint lines. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which bones form the ankle region?","choices":["The femur, patella, and tibia","The tibia, fibula, and talus","The radius, ulna, and carpals","The humerus, scapula, and clavicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ankle is formed by the tibia, fibula, and talus, articulating at the tibiotalar and subtalar joints. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Normal ankle dorsiflexion is approximately what range?","choices":["About 35 to 50 degrees","About 70 to 90 degrees","About 110 degrees","About 10 to 20 degrees"],"answer":3,"exp":"Normal ankle dorsiflexion is approximately 10 to 20 degrees, while plantar flexion is about 35 to 50 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Turning the sole of the foot inward, toward the midline, is which ankle movement?","choices":["Eversion","Inversion","Dorsiflexion","Plantar flexion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Turning the sole of the foot inward toward the midline is inversion, normally about 30 degrees; turning it outward is eversion. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"The anterior talofibular ligament, calcaneofibular ligament, and posterior talofibular ligament are ligaments of which region?","choices":["The medial knee","The lateral ankle","The wrist","The cervical spine"],"answer":1,"exp":"The anterior talofibular, calcaneofibular, and posterior talofibular ligaments are the lateral ankle ligaments. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which thumb movement is tested by having the patient touch the thumb to the tip of the little finger?","choices":["Thumb abduction","Thumb opposition","Thumb extension","Thumb adduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thumb opposition is tested by having the patient touch the thumb to the tip of the pinky (little finger). — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 47"},{"q":"Normal wrist flexion, bringing the fingers toward the floor with palms down, is approximately what range?","choices":["About 75 to 90 degrees","About 20 to 25 degrees","About 5 to 10 degrees","About 130 degrees"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal wrist flexion, bringing the fingers toward the floor with palms down, is approximately 75 to 90 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"Bringing the fingers toward the midline with the palms down tests which wrist movement?","choices":["Wrist hyperextension","Radial deviation","Ulnar deviation","Finger abduction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bringing the fingers toward the midline with palms down is radial deviation, normally about 20 to 25 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 45"},{"q":"When the elbow is held at 90 degrees, turning the palm down and then up (as if carrying a bowl of soup) tests what?","choices":["Flexion and extension","Abduction and adduction","Internal and external rotation","Pronation and supination"],"answer":3,"exp":"With the elbow at 90 degrees, turning the palm down tests pronation and turning it up tests supination. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Where is the ulnar nerve palpated at the elbow?","choices":["Anteriorly, over the bicipital groove of the arm","Posteriorly, between the olecranon process and the medial epicondyle","At the radial styloid process of the wrist","Over the lateral malleolus of the ankle joint"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ulnar nerve is palpated posteriorly at the elbow, in the groove between the olecranon process and the medial epicondyle. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 40"},{"q":"The sternoclavicular joint is formed by which structures?","choices":["The medial end of the clavicle with the upper sternum","The lateral clavicle with the acromion","The humeral head with the glenoid fossa","The scapular spine with the first rib"],"answer":0,"exp":"The sternoclavicular joint is formed by the medial end of the clavicle articulating with the upper sternum. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which six maneuvers are used to test shoulder range of motion?","choices":["Pronation, supination, inversion, eversion, flexion, extension","Dorsiflexion, plantar flexion, radial and ulnar deviation, flexion, extension","Flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, internal rotation, external rotation","Protrusion, retraction, elevation, depression, rotation, tilt"],"answer":2,"exp":"Shoulder range of motion is tested with six maneuvers: flexion, extension, adduction, abduction, internal rotation, and external rotation. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"The knee and the temporomandibular joint are examples of which synovial joint type?","choices":["Spheroidal (ball-and-socket) joints","Hinge joints","Condylar joints","Fibrous joints"],"answer":2,"exp":"The knee and temporomandibular joint are condylar joints, in which the two articulating surfaces move together and are not dissociable. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"During the general survey, observing how the patient walks into the room assesses what — which is also part of the neuro exam?","choices":["Deep tendon reflexes","Pupillary reactions","Blood pressure","Gait (how the patient walks)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Observing how the patient moves and walks assesses gait, which is part of both the general musculoskeletal survey and the neurological exam. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"Cervical lateral bending, bringing the ear toward the shoulder, occurs mainly at which levels?","choices":["The skull and C1 only","The C2 through C7 levels","The C1 to C2 level only","The thoracic T4 to T8 levels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cervical lateral bending, bringing the ear toward the shoulder (normally about 40 degrees), occurs mainly at the C2 through C7 levels. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Before evaluating the thoracic and lumbar spine, the examiner inspects the pelvis for symmetry of which landmarks?","choices":["The iliac crests and posterior superior iliac spines","The acromion processes and clavicles","The medial malleoli and heels","The mastoid processes and tragus"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvis is inspected for symmetry in the level of the iliac crests and the posterior superior iliac spines, since the pelvis is the base of the spine. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"A muscle that moves through full range of motion only when gravity is eliminated is graded as what?","choices":["Grade 1","Grade 2","Grade 4","Grade 5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Grade 2 muscle strength is full range of motion but not against gravity — movement only when gravity is eliminated. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"Grade 4 muscle strength is best described as what?","choices":["No evidence of any muscle movement","Full muscle strength against complete applied resistance","Only a trace of muscle movement","Full range of motion against gravity and some resistance, but weak"],"answer":3,"exp":"Grade 4 muscle strength is full range of motion against gravity and some resistance, but weaker than normal. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 18"},{"q":"A grinding, crunching, or crackling felt or heard during joint movement is called what?","choices":["Effusion","Fasciculation","Crepitus","Atrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Crepitus is a crunching, crackling, or grinding sensation heard or felt during joint movement, noted during range-of-motion testing. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"The nucleus pulposus, which cushions bony movement, is found within what?","choices":["The synovial cavity of a ball-and-socket joint","A fluid-filled bursa over a tendon","The joint capsule of a hinge joint","The fibrocartilaginous discs of cartilaginous joints"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nucleus pulposus is contained within the fibrocartilaginous discs of cartilaginous joints and cushions bony movement. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 7"},{"q":"Normal elbow extension is closest to which value?","choices":["About 0 to negative 5 degrees","About 90 degrees of remaining flexion","About 160 degrees","About 45 degrees"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal elbow extension is about 0 to negative 5 degrees — the arm straightens fully or slightly past straight. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which joints does the ankle region include?","choices":["The tibiotalar joint and the subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint","The glenohumeral and acromioclavicular joints","The radiocarpal and midcarpal joints","The atlanto-occipital and atlantoaxial joints"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ankle includes the tibiotalar joint and the subtalar (talocalcaneal) joint. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"Normal finger flexion, making a fist, is approximately how many degrees at the joints?","choices":["About 90 degrees","About 20 degrees","About 45 degrees","About 160 degrees"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal finger flexion, making a fist with the thumb across the knuckles, is approximately 90 degrees, with finger extension about 30 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 46"},{"q":"Normal ankle plantar flexion, pointing the foot downward, is approximately what range?","choices":["About 35 to 50 degrees","About 10 to 20 degrees total","About 5 degrees at most","About 90 degrees or more"],"answer":0,"exp":"Normal ankle plantar flexion, pointing the foot downward, is approximately 35 to 50 degrees, while dorsiflexion is about 10 to 20 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which maneuver tests rotation of the thoracic and lumbar spine?","choices":["Trying to touch the toes forward","Bending straight back as far as possible","Rotating from side to side at the waist","Bringing one ear toward the shoulder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Thoracolumbar rotation, normally about 3 to 18 degrees, is tested by having the patient rotate from side to side at the waist while the hips are stabilized. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 30"}]};
